  imagePosition="75% center"
>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters are often considered for the same rooms.</p>
  <p>Both are quiet electric resistance heaters commonly used in <a href="/categories/bedroom/">bedrooms</a>, <a href="/categories/home-office/">home offices</a> and <a href="/categories/living-spaces/">smaller living areas</a>. Neither relies on a strong fan to distribute heat, and both can provide steady background warmth rather than an immediate blast of hot air.</p>
  <p>The practical differences are in how they warm the room, how quickly the effect is noticed, how much floor or wall space they require and how easily they can be moved or stored.</p>
  <p>Their slim or silent operation should not be confused with low electricity use or suitability for unattended heating.</p>
  <p>This guide explains the differences to check before choosing either type.</p>
  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>Choose according to the room and the way it is occupied.</p>
  <p>An oil column heater can suit a room that needs quiet, gradual warmth and where a portable appliance can remain safely positioned. A panel heater can suit a smaller enclosed room where wall mounting and a shallow profile are more useful than portability.</p>
  <p>Neither type will perform well when expected to heat a large open-plan area, several connected rooms or a space losing heat through persistent draughts.</p>
  <p>Compare rated power, thermostat behaviour, placement and controls rather than choosing from appearance alone.</p>
  <h2>What is an oil column heater?</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater is a portable electric resistance heater containing sealed heat-transfer oil.</p>
  <p>An electrical element warms the oil inside the appliance. Heat then moves through the metal columns and into the room through a combination of natural convection and radiant warmth.</p>
  <p>The oil:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>remains sealed inside the heater</li>
    <li>is not burned as fuel</li>
    <li>is not normally consumed</li>
    <li>does not require routine topping up</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The heated metal body continues releasing some warmth after the electrical element cycles off, although it also takes time to warm initially.</p>
  <p>Most models include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>castors or feet</li>
    <li>a thermostat</li>
    <li>several heat settings</li>
    <li>a timer or digital controls on some models</li>
    <li>cord storage</li>
    <li>overheat protection</li>
    <li>a tip-over switch on some models</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Their main characteristics are quiet operation, gradual warm-up and a relatively bulky portable body.</p>
  <h2>What is a panel heater?</h2>
  <p>A panel heater is a slim electric resistance heater designed primarily to warm air through natural convection.</p>
  <p>Cooler room air enters around the lower part of the heater, warms inside or behind the panel and rises into the room.</p>
  <p>Depending on the model, it may be:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>permanently wall-mounted</li>
    <li>mounted on removable brackets</li>
    <li>used as a portable floor unit with compatible feet</li>
    <li>controlled mechanically or digitally</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Panel heaters are generally quiet because most do not use a fan.</p>
  <p>Their shallow design can make them easier to place in a bedroom, hallway or compact office where floor space is limited.</p>
  <p>The slim body does not prove low power consumption. Check the rated input in watts and how the thermostat controls the element.</p>
  <h2>Oil column and panel heater comparison</h2>
