  <h2>How the heat feels</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater produces a combination of radiant and convective warmth.</p>
  <p>People sitting relatively close may notice warmth from the metal body before the whole room reaches a stable temperature. Heated air also rises through and around the columns and circulates gradually.</p>
  <p>A panel heater relies more heavily on convection. Warm air rises from the heater and mixes with cooler room air.</p>
  <p>This can produce a less direct sensation than an oil column heater, particularly when first switched on.</p>
  <p>Neither type usually provides the immediate effect of a fan heater or radiant bar heater.</p>
  <p>They are more suited to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>allowing an enclosed room to warm gradually</li>
    <li>maintaining a moderate temperature</li>
    <li>quiet supervised use</li>
    <li>spaces where strong fan noise or airflow is undesirable</li>
  </ul>
  <p>They are less suited to ten minutes of rapid warmth before leaving the room.</p>
  <h2>Which warms the room faster?</h2>
  <p>The answer depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>rated power</li>
    <li>room size</li>
    <li>ceiling height</li>
    <li>starting temperature</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>draughts</li>
    <li>window area</li>
    <li>thermostat setting</li>
    <li>heater position</li>
    <li>whether the door remains closed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An oil column heater must first warm its internal oil and metal body. This can make the initial response feel slow.</p>
  <p>A panel heater may begin warming rising air earlier, but it still relies on gradual room circulation.</p>
  <p>A panel heater is not automatically fast simply because its surface is thin.</p>
  <p>Where immediate warmth is the priority, a <a href="/guides/fan-heater-vs-ceramic-heater/">fan-assisted heater</a> may be more suitable for short supervised use. Where quiet, steady warmth matters more, either an oil column or panel heater may be appropriate.</p>
  <h2>Does an oil column heater stay warm longer?</h2>
  <p>The oil and metal body retain some heat after the electrical element switches off.</p>
  <p>This allows the heater to continue releasing warmth for a period while it cools.</p>
  <p>That does not create free heat.</p>
  <p>The stored warmth came from electricity already used by the element. Thermal mass changes when the heat enters the room; it does not increase the total heat created from the electrical input.</p>
  <p>This retained warmth can make temperature changes feel gradual and reduce the sharp on-off sensation of some lightweight heaters.</p>
  <p>It does not mean an oil column heater is inherently cheaper to operate than every panel heater.</p>
  <h2>Are panel heaters cheaper to run?</h2>
  <p>Not automatically.</p>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters are both electric resistance heaters.</p>
  <p>At the point of use, electric resistance heaters convert electrical energy into heat. Electricity consumption is primarily determined by:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>rated input power</li>
    <li>selected heat setting</li>
    <li>how long the element operates</li>
    <li>thermostat cycling</li>
    <li>target temperature</li>
    <li>room heat loss</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If a 2000-watt oil column heater and a 2000-watt panel heater both operate continuously at full input for the same period, their electricity consumption will be broadly similar.</p>
  <p>Differences over a normal session may result from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>thermostat accuracy</li>
    <li>heater position</li>
    <li>how evenly the room warms</li>
    <li>lower heat settings</li>
    <li>control schedules</li>
    <li>the user’s preferred temperature</li>
    <li>how often the element cycles</li>
    <li>whether one heater is better suited to the space</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not infer electricity use from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>slimness</li>
    <li>the word “eco”</li>
    <li>the presence of oil</li>
    <li>surface temperature</li>
    <li>wall mounting</li>
    <li>the number of columns</li>
    <li>marketing claims without rated input information</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check the wattage and intended operating pattern.</p>
  <h2>Rated power and heat settings</h2>
  <p>Portable and wall-mounted resistance heaters commonly use power ratings expressed in watts.</p>
  <p>A heater may provide:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>one fixed heat level</li>
    <li>two or three stepped heat settings</li>
    <li>digital control of the element</li>
    <li>an automatic or economy mode</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A lower setting generally reduces the heater’s electrical input and heat output.</p>
  <p>It does not make the process fundamentally more efficient. It simply provides less heat while operating at that setting.</p>
  <p>Check whether the stated maximum power applies to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the entire heater</li>
    <li>one selected setting</li>
    <li>a boost mode</li>
    <li>a fan-assisted mode where fitted</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The heater must still be capable of warming the room. A low setting that runs continuously without reaching a comfortable temperature may not provide the intended result.</p>
  <h2>Thermostat quality matters</h2>
  <p>The thermostat controls when the heating element switches on and off.</p>
  <p>A basic mechanical thermostat may use an unnumbered dial. The user allows the room to warm, then reduces the setting until the heater cycles.</p>
  <p>Digital heaters may allow a target temperature to be selected.</p>
  <p>Neither approach guarantees precise room-temperature control.</p>
  <p>The sensor may be affected by:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>its position within the heater</li>
    <li>warm air collecting around the appliance</li>
    <li>a cold external wall</li>
    <li>direct sun</li>
    <li>nearby door openings</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>furniture</li>
    <li>room airflow</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Useful controls may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>clear temperature settings</li>
    <li>a timer</li>
    <li>daily scheduling</li>
    <li>child-control lock</li>
    <li>frost-protection mode</li>
    <li>memory after power interruption</li>
    <li>a clear display</li>
    <li>simple manual override</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check what happens after electricity is interrupted. Automatic restarting may be undesirable where the heater could resume without supervision.</p>
  <h2>Noise</h2>
  <p>Both types are generally quieter than fan-assisted heaters.</p>
  <p>Possible oil column sounds include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>thermostat clicks</li>
    <li>minor ticking or expansion sounds</li>
    <li>movement from loose castors</li>
    <li>electronic-control sounds</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Possible panel-heater sounds include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>thermostat clicks</li>
    <li>expansion noises</li>
    <li>electronic relays</li>
    <li>control beeps</li>
    <li>vibration against wall brackets</li>
    <li>fan noise if the model includes a boost fan</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No portable or fixed appliance should be assumed completely silent.</p>
  <p>For bedrooms and home offices, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>low-temperature cycling noise</li>
    <li>display brightness</li>
    <li>control beeps</li>
    <li>relay clicks</li>
    <li>expansion noises after switch-on</li>
    <li>whether sounds can be disabled</li>
    <li>whether wall mounting transfers vibration</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A quiet showroom may not reveal sounds that become noticeable at night.</p>
  <h2>Room size and layout</h2>
  <p>Both heater types generally make more sense in small or moderate enclosed rooms.</p>
  <p>They may struggle when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the room opens into a hallway</li>
    <li>several rooms are connected without doors</li>
    <li>ceilings are high</li>
    <li>windows are large and poorly covered</li>
    <li>external doors open frequently</li>
    <li>the room is poorly insulated</li>
    <li>significant draughts remain</li>
    <li>the heater is blocked by furniture</li>
    <li>the home has very cold outdoor conditions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not rely solely on a broad room-size statement printed on packaging.</p>
  <p>Measure:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>length</li>
    <li>width</li>
    <li>ceiling height</li>
    <li>connected spaces</li>
    <li>major windows</li>
    <li>external doors</li>
    <li>usable wall and floor positions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Manufacturer room guidance is based on assumptions that may not match a draughty or highly glazed room.</p>
  <h2>Oil column heater placement</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater requires a stable floor position and safe clearance on every side.</p>
  <p>Before buying, identify a location that is:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>flat and level</li>
    <li>directly accessible to a suitable wall power point</li>
    <li>outside the walking path</li>
    <li>away from curtains</li>
    <li>away from bedding</li>
    <li>away from upholstered furniture</li>
    <li>away from drying clothes</li>
    <li>away from paper and storage</li>
    <li>protected from children or pets knocking it</li>
    <li>clear of doors</li>
    <li>clear of water</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Its castors make it movable, but it should not be wheeled by pulling the electrical cord.</p>
  <p>Do not place it:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>beside a bed</li>
    <li>beneath a desk filled with cables or paper</li>
    <li>directly behind a door</li>
    <li>beneath hanging laundry</li>
    <li>on unstable furniture</li>
    <li>where a rug interferes with stability or clearance</li>
    <li>in a bathroom unless specifically designed and installed for that environment</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The columns can remain hot after switch-off. Allow the heater to cool before moving or storing it.</p>
  <h2>Panel-heater placement</h2>
  <p>A panel heater may preserve floor space when properly wall-mounted.</p>
  <p>The proposed wall must still provide:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>manufacturer-required clearance above and below</li>
    <li>clearance from curtains and furniture</li>
    <li>clear convection airflow</li>
    <li>access to controls</li>
    <li>access to a suitable power supply</li>
    <li>secure mounting</li>
    <li>a position outside splash and wet areas</li>
    <li>access for removal or servicing</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not mount a panel heater:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>behind a sofa</li>
    <li>behind a desk</li>
    <li>below floor-length curtains</li>
    <li>beneath shelves</li>
    <li>beside hanging clothes</li>
    <li>where a door covers it</li>
    <li>where children can insert objects into openings</li>
    <li>on a surface not permitted by the manufacturer</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Blocking the lower inlet or upper outlet can reduce performance and create overheating.</p>
  <p>A wall-mounted appearance does not make the heater part of the building unless it is installed and connected in the manner specified.</p>
  <h2>Wall mounting, renters and electrical work</h2>
  <p>Some panel heaters plug into a normal wall power point after being attached to manufacturer-supplied brackets. Others may require fixed electrical installation.</p>
  <p>Do not assume all models are installed the same way.</p>
  <p>Before purchasing, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>whether wall brackets are included</li>
    <li>wall material</li>
    <li>fixing requirements</li>
    <li>minimum clearances</li>
    <li>power-point position</li>
    <li>whether the cord remains visible</li>
    <li>whether hard-wiring is required</li>
    <li>whether wall marking or drilling is acceptable</li>
    <li>whether installation affects the warranty</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Renters should obtain written approval before drilling, permanently mounting a heater or altering electrical fittings.</p>
  <p>Any fixed electrical work must be completed by an appropriately licensed electrician under the requirements that apply in the relevant state or territory.</p>
  <p>Do not cut off a plug, conceal a flexible cord inside a wall or improvise an electrical connection.</p>
  <h2>Portability and storage</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater is designed to move between suitable rooms, but it can be heavy and awkward.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>assembled weight</li>
    <li>quality of castors</li>
    <li>handle position</li>
    <li>whether stairs are involved</li>
    <li>cord storage</li>
    <li>storage footprint</li>
    <li>whether it can be moved without touching hot columns</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A portable panel heater may be lighter, but stability depends on the supplied feet and the floor surface.</p>
  <p>Do not use:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>improvised feet</li>
    <li>a wall-mounted heater standing loose on the floor</li>
    <li>a portable heater without all supplied supports</li>
    <li>a damaged castor or bracket</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before summer storage:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>switch the heater off</li>
    <li>unplug it</li>
    <li>allow it to cool completely</li>
    <li>inspect the cord and plug</li>
    <li>clean only as instructed</li>
    <li>secure the cord without sharp bends</li>
    <li>store it upright where specified</li>
    <li>keep heavy items off it</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Bedrooms and sleep</h2>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters are often marketed towards bedrooms because they operate quietly.</p>
  <p>Quiet operation does not make a heater suitable for unattended use or operation while people sleep.</p>
  <p>Fire and Rescue NSW advises switching heaters off before leaving the room or going to bed.</p>
  <p>Do not rely on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a thermostat</li>
    <li>a timer</li>
    <li>tip-over protection</li>
    <li>overheat protection</li>
    <li>a cool-touch description</li>
    <li>silent operation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>as permission to ignore current safety guidance or manufacturer instructions.</p>
  <p>Before bedtime:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>warm the room while it is occupied and supervised</li>
    <li>turn the heater off</li>
    <li>confirm nearby bedding remains clear</li>
    <li>avoid leaving clothes or towels near the heater</li>
    <li>keep working smoke alarms maintained</li>
    <li>use bedding suitable for the conditions</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A room that becomes dangerously cold or requires continuous overnight heating may need a more appropriate fixed heating assessment rather than reliance on a portable resistance heater.</p>
  <h2>Children and pets</h2>
  <p>Both heater types create hot surfaces and require clear space.</p>
  <p>Consider whether children or pets may:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>touch the heater</li>
    <li>push toys against it</li>
    <li>pull the power cord</li>
    <li>climb on it</li>
    <li>cover it with clothing or bedding</li>
    <li>block convection openings</li>
    <li>knock it over</li>
    <li>move furniture into the clearance zone</li>
    <li>chew cables</li>
  </ul>
  <p>An oil column heater can remain hot after it is switched off.</p>
  <p>A panel heater may appear less hazardous because it is flat and wall-mounted, but its outlet and surrounding surfaces can still become hot.</p>
  <p>Supervise children and pets whenever a heater is operating.</p>
  <p>A child-control lock prevents control changes; it does not prevent physical contact or obstruction.</p>
  <h2>Surface temperatures</h2>
  <p>Oil column heaters often have lower-temperature surfaces than exposed radiant bar heaters, but the columns can still become hot enough to cause discomfort or injury.</p>
  <p>Panel heaters may be advertised as low-surface-temperature or cool-touch, but those terms depend on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the model</li>
    <li>measured surface</li>
    <li>operating setting</li>
    <li>room conditions</li>
    <li>product test method</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The outlet and surrounding areas may still be hotter than the main front panel.</p>
  <p>Do not use a surface-temperature description as permission to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>touch the heater</li>
    <li>place it near children</li>
    <li>dry clothes on it</li>
    <li>cover it</li>
    <li>reduce clearances</li>
    <li>place furniture against it</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Follow the product’s specific instructions.</p>
  <h2>Safety features</h2>
  <p>Common features include:</p>
  <h3>Overheat protection</h3>
  <p>This interrupts operation if internal temperatures become excessive.</p>
  <p>It is backup protection, not a substitute for clear airflow and correct placement.</p>
  <h3>Tip-over shut-off</h3>
  <p>This is particularly relevant to portable heaters.</p>
  <p>Check whether the feature is present and whether the heater remains stable on the intended floor.</p>
  <h3>Timer</h3>
  <p>A timer can limit operation but should not be used to justify unattended or sleeping use.</p>
  <h3>Child lock</h3>
  <p>A child lock can prevent accidental control changes but cannot prevent contact, covering or movement.</p>
  <h3>Frost-protection mode</h3>
  <p>This may activate the heater at a low room temperature.</p>
  <p>Understand whether it can start automatically and whether the proposed use is compatible with supervision and product-safety guidance.</p>
  <p>No safety feature removes the need to inspect the heater and maintain clearances.</p>
  <h2>Power points and electrical load</h2>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters are high-powered appliances.</p>
  <p>Current Product Safety guidance recommends only one appliance per power point.</p>
  <p>Fire and Rescue NSW advises plugging heaters directly into a wall power point rather than a power board.</p>
  <p>Before buying, check that the heater can reach a suitable wall outlet without:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>stretching the cord</li>
    <li>crossing a walkway</li>
    <li>passing beneath a rug</li>
    <li>running through a doorway</li>
    <li>being trapped behind furniture</li>
    <li>using a double adaptor</li>
    <li>overloading a power board</li>
    <li>joining extension leads</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Inspect the wall outlet, plug and cord.</p>
  <p>Stop using the heater if there is:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>discolouration</li>
    <li>cracking</li>
    <li>exposed wiring</li>
    <li>a loose plug</li>
    <li>unusual heat</li>
    <li>sparking</li>
    <li>a burning smell</li>
    <li>intermittent power</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A damaged or unsuitable wall outlet should be assessed by a licensed electrician.</p>
  <h2>One-metre clearance</h2>
  <p>Current Product Safety and Fire and Rescue NSW guidance recommends keeping combustible materials at least one metre from heaters.</p>
  <p>This includes:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>clothing</li>
    <li>bedding</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>furniture</li>
    <li>lounges</li>
    <li>throws</li>
    <li>drying racks</li>
    <li>paper</li>
    <li>towels</li>
    <li>pet bedding</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The manufacturer may specify additional or different clearances for particular parts of the heater.</p>
  <p>Use the greater requirement where applicable.</p>
  <p>Do not assume a wall-mounted panel heater can operate behind furniture because it does not occupy the floor.</p>
  <h2>Do not dry clothes on either heater</h2>
  <p>Oil column heaters can appear convenient for warming or drying clothes because of their vertical fins.</p>
  <p>Do not drape clothing, towels or bedding over them.</p>
  <p>Covering a heater can:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>trap heat</li>
    <li>obstruct airflow</li>
    <li>cause overheating</li>
    <li>damage the appliance</li>
    <li>ignite combustible material</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not place a drying rack inside the required clearance zone.</p>
  <p>The same applies to panel heaters. Do not hang damp laundry above, against or immediately in front of the heater.</p>
  <p>Use an appropriate drying method with suitable ventilation.</p>
  <h2>Condensation and ventilation</h2>
  <p>Both heater types add dry electrical heat rather than combustion moisture.</p>
  <p>They do not remove moisture already inside the home.</p>
  <p>Condensation may still form where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>indoor humidity is high</li>
    <li>windows and external walls remain cold</li>
    <li>clothes dry indoors</li>
    <li>bathrooms or kitchens are poorly exhausted</li>
    <li>air circulation is limited</li>
    <li>furniture sits tightly against cold walls</li>
    <li>building leaks are present</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not respond to condensation by sealing every opening or running a larger heater indefinitely.</p>
  <p>Use appropriate ventilation, exhaust moisture at its source and investigate recurring dampness or mould.</p>
  <h2>When an oil column heater may suit</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater may suit when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>quiet operation is important</li>
    <li>gradual background warmth is acceptable</li>
    <li>the room is small or moderately sized and enclosed</li>
    <li>floor space is available</li>
    <li>the heater may need to move between suitable rooms</li>
    <li>retained warmth after cycling is preferred</li>
    <li>strong fan airflow is undesirable</li>
    <li>the heater can remain supervised and safely positioned</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It may be less suitable when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>immediate heat is required</li>
    <li>floor space is limited</li>
    <li>stairs make movement difficult</li>
    <li>children or pets can readily reach it</li>
    <li>the room is large or open</li>
    <li>storage space is limited</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>When a panel heater may suit</h2>
  <p>A panel heater may suit when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>a slim profile is useful</li>
    <li>wall mounting is permitted</li>
    <li>floor space needs to remain clear</li>
    <li>quiet background heating is preferred</li>
    <li>the room is enclosed</li>
    <li>controls and scheduling suit the intended supervised use</li>
    <li>the wall position allows clear convection airflow</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It may be less suitable when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the only wall is behind furniture or curtains</li>
    <li>the home is rented and mounting is not approved</li>
    <li>the room is large or draughty</li>
    <li>rapid heat is required</li>
    <li>electrical installation is more complex than expected</li>
    <li>portability matters</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>When neither is the right choice</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/home-heating-options-australia/">Another heating option</a> may be more suitable when:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the room needs fast heat for a short period</li>
    <li>a large open-plan area needs regular heating</li>
    <li>several rooms need conditioning</li>
    <li>the home also needs summer cooling</li>
    <li>the heater would operate for long periods every day</li>
    <li>no safe floor or wall position exists</li>
    <li>draughts and heat loss remain uncontrolled</li>
    <li>overnight temperature management is required</li>
    <li><a href="/seasonal/">local winter conditions</a> demand a more capable fixed system</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A correctly sized <a href="/guides/air-conditioning-buying-guide/">reverse-cycle air conditioner</a> can provide heating and cooling and is substantially more efficient than electric resistance heating for regular room conditioning.</p>
  <p>A fan heater may be more practical for brief supervised use.</p>
  <p>An <a href="/guides/electric-blankets-vs-heated-throws/">electric blanket or heated throw</a> may suit personal warmth without heating the entire room.</p>
  <h2>Improve the room first</h2>
  <p>Before purchasing a larger resistance heater, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">ceiling insulation</a></li>
    <li><a href="/guides/bedroom-lighting-and-window-coverings-what-to-compare-before-you-buy/">window coverings</a></li>
    <li>gaps around external doors</li>
    <li>worn window seals</li>
    <li>unused rooms left open</li>
    <li>cold hard floors</li>
    <li>seating beside large windows</li>
    <li>curtains blocked by furniture</li>
    <li>ceiling fans with a suitable winter mode</li>
    <li>existing reverse-cycle equipment</li>
    <li>room ventilation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Close doors to unoccupied rooms where practical.</p>
  <p>Open suitable curtains to useful winter sun, then close them before outdoor temperatures fall.</p>
  <p>A rug can improve comfort over a cold hard floor but must remain outside the heater’s clearance zone.</p>
  <p>Reducing heat loss can improve the performance of either heater without increasing its rated power.</p>
  <h2>Before You Buy Checklist</h2>
  <p>Before choosing an oil column or panel heater, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>What are the room dimensions?</li>
    <li>What is the ceiling height?</li>
    <li>Is the room enclosed?</li>
    <li>Are hallways or adjoining rooms permanently open?</li>
    <li>How well is the room insulated?</li>
    <li>Are there large or poorly covered windows?</li>
    <li>Are significant draughts present?</li>
    <li>Is rapid heat or steady background warmth required?</li>
    <li>How many hours will the heater operate at a time?</li>
    <li>Is fan-free quiet operation important?</li>
    <li>What is the heater’s rated input power?</li>
    <li>What power applies to each heat setting?</li>
    <li>Does the thermostat cycle effectively?</li>
    <li>Does the heater restart automatically after a power interruption?</li>
    <li>Are timer and scheduling controls genuinely useful?</li>
    <li>Is control-panel brightness or noise important at night?</li>
    <li>Is there a safe floor position for an oil column heater?</li>
    <li>Is the floor level and stable?</li>
    <li>Can the oil heater be moved and stored safely?</li>
    <li>Is there a suitable wall for a panel heater?</li>
    <li>Can the required wall clearances be maintained?</li>
    <li>Will furniture or curtains obstruct the panel?</li>
    <li>Is wall mounting permitted?</li>
    <li>Does mounting require landlord approval?</li>
    <li>Does the panel plug in or require fixed electrical work?</li>
    <li>Are manufacturer mounting brackets included?</li>
    <li>Is there a suitable wall power point?</li>
    <li>Can the heater plug directly into the wall?</li>
    <li>Can one appliance be used per power point?</li>
    <li>Can the cord avoid walkways and rugs?</li>
    <li>Can at least one metre be maintained from combustible items?</li>
    <li>Are children or pets likely to touch, move or cover the heater?</li>
    <li>Are overheat and tip-over protections provided where relevant?</li>
    <li>Can convection inlets and outlets remain unobstructed?</li>
    <li>Will the heater be switched off before leaving the room or going to bed?</li>
    <li>Have working smoke alarms been maintained?</li>
    <li>Has the model been checked against the Product Safety recalls database?</li>
    <li>Would draught control or better window coverings solve part of the problem?</li>
    <li>Would reverse-cycle heating better suit regular long-duration use?</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Choose for the room, not the shape</h2>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters can both provide quiet, gradual warmth in a suitable enclosed room.</p>
  <p>An oil column heater offers portability, a combination of radiant and convective warmth and some retained heat after its element cycles off. A panel heater offers a shallow profile and can preserve floor space when correctly wall-mounted.</p>
  <p>Neither design is inherently cheap to run. Both are electric resistance heaters, so rated power, operating time, thermostat control and room heat loss determine electricity use.</p>
  <p>Measure the space, identify a safe position and check how the heater will be controlled and supervised.</p>
  <p>The better choice is the one that suits the room without blocking airflow, occupying an unsafe position or working continuously against draughts and poor insulation.</p>

